0) WHY THIS TOPIC EXISTS IN REAL SYSTEMS
===============================================================================

Before Raft writes, ask the interviewer:
“How do multiple machines agree on the SAME ordered history of writes even when
machines crash, networks fail, or messages arrive late?”

This is the heart of:
- WhatsApp message ordering
- Payment ledger correctness
- Uber driver-trip state transitions
- Google Docs collaboration metadata
- Amazon inventory reservation
- Netflix control plane configs
- Kubernetes leader state
- Metadata systems like HDFS NameNode HA
- Databases like CockroachDB, TiKV, Yugabyte internals

WHY this exists:
In distributed systems, a single machine is never enough because:
- machine crash risk
- region outage
- scale requirements
- maintenance downtime
- read scalability
- durability needs

But once we replicate data, NEW problem appears:
“How do replicas agree on exactly WHICH write happened and in WHAT order?”

That exact problem = Distributed Consensus.

Raft solves:
- leader election
- write ordering
- replicated logs
- safe failover
- recovery after crashes
- stale leader protection

This video specifically focuses on:
- how WRITES happen
- log replication
- backfilling missing logs
- prefix/suffix understanding
- why this is fault tolerant but slower
- why it does NOT replace 2PC

===============================================================================
1) INTUITION
===============================================================================

Think of Raft like a classroom notebook system.

Teacher = Leader
Students = Followers
Homework entries = Logs / Writes

Every new homework instruction:
1. Teacher writes in master notebook
2. Students copy it
3. Majority students confirm copied
4. Only then instruction becomes official

Even if one student is absent:
- class still continues
- absent student later copies missing pages
- this is BACKFILL

This simple classroom analogy is the entire intuition of Raft writes.

KEY IDEA:
A write is not “done” when leader receives it.
A write is “done” only when majority replicas persist it.

This gives:
- fault tolerance
- durability
- linearizable leader writes
- safe failover

