2) REAL LIFE ANALOGY
===============================================================================

PAYMENT SYSTEM:
Suppose user transfers ₹500 from wallet to merchant.

If only one server writes:
- machine crash = lost money state

So we replicate across 5 replicas:
- Leader logs “Debit ₹500”
- Replicas copy
- 3/5 acknowledge
- commit success

Now even if leader dies:
majority still has durable history.

This is why financial ledgers love consensus-backed replication.

WITHOUT this:
double debit, ghost payment, stale balance.

===============================================================================
3) CORE IDEA — RAFT WRITES
===============================================================================

A write in Raft follows this pipeline:

CLIENT → LEADER → FOLLOWERS → MAJORITY ACK → COMMIT → APPLY → RESPONSE

Step-by-step:

(1) Client sends write
Example:
SET balance = 500

(2) Leader appends to its log
log[10] = (term=5, command=SET balance=500)

(3) Leader sends AppendEntries RPC to followers

(4) Followers append if log matches previous prefix

(5) Once majority ACK:
entry becomes COMMITTED

(6) Leader applies to state machine

(7) Followers later apply after commit index update

IMPORTANT:
Replication happens BEFORE user success response.

This is why Raft writes are slower than single-node writes.

