9) WHY RAFT WRITES FEEL SLOW
===============================================================================

Jordan’s conclusion here is very important.

WHY slow:
A single write waits for:
- local WAL
- network fanout
- follower WAL
- majority ACK
- commit
- response

Latency ≈
leader fsync +
network RTT +
slow quorum follower fsync

Cross-region gets even slower.

Example:
Single node DB = 2 ms
Raft quorum = 8–25 ms
Cross region = 50–150 ms+

Tradeoff:
SLOWER BUT SAFER

This is classic latency vs fault tolerance tradeoff.

CAP view:
During partitions, Raft prefers CP.
Consistency over availability.

===============================================================================
10) FAILURE SCENARIOS
===============================================================================

A) LEADER MACHINE CRASH
-----------------------------------
Majority followers still hold committed logs.
Election happens.
New leader continues safely.

B) FOLLOWER CRASH
-----------------------------------
Writes continue if majority remains.

C) NETWORK PARTITION
-----------------------------------
Minority side cannot elect leader.
Prevents split brain.

WHY:
Majority quorum requirement.

D) STALE LEADER
-----------------------------------
Old leader isolated.
Cannot commit without majority.
Safe by design.

E) DUPLICATE CLIENT RETRIES
-----------------------------------
Use request idempotency token.
Otherwise duplicate ledger writes possible.

F) REGION FAILURE
-----------------------------------
If quorum spans 3 AZs:
1 AZ loss tolerated.

For region-level:
multi-region quorum needed
but latency huge.

===============================================================================
11) SPLIT BRAIN PREVENTION
===============================================================================

WHY it matters:
Two leaders = divergent truths.

Raft prevents this via:
- term numbers
- majority election
- log up-to-date rule

Only node with most recent committed prefix can win.

This is huge interviewer follow-up.

Golden line:
“Raft prevents split brain by combining majority quorum with term-based
leadership and log freshness constraints.”

